    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d41">

                <pb facs="0148" n="52" xml:id="pg52"/>

                <head>No. 30.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Kasson to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-03-11"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 11, 1879</date>.
                        (Received March 27.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 173.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: An official summary of the commercial
                    movement for the year 1878 has been published. It shows a total value of imports
                    of 579,547,828 florins, or in round numbers $278,000,000, and a total value of
                    exports of 698,302,513 florins, or in round numbers of $335,000,000, Thus the
                    balance of trade appears to have been in favor of Austria-Hungary by about
                    $57,000,000 for the year 1878.</p>
                <p>The increase of imports over the amount reported for the year 1877 is 24,320,780
                    florins. The increase of exports is 36,270,309 florins.</p>
                <p>These results indicate an amelioration of the financial condition of the empire
                    and of its people.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN A. KASSON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
